\begin{abstract}
Autonomous research agents can design and optimize machine learning systems, but the goals we give them shape what they discover. We introduce AutoPareto, a multi-objective research framework built on Andrej Karpathy's autoresearch, in which coding agents modify a Transformer's architecture, its training procedure and, in later campaigns, its inference code. Rather than optimizing language modelling quality alone, AutoPareto scores quality, generation speed and memory, and every candidate trains for the same fixed GPU time. A faster program therefore also trains on more data. Across independent campaigns, agents from two model families discovered that smaller Transformers and smaller batches fit substantially more training into the same time. Starting from a model with 50 million parameters, AutoPareto found a 29-million-parameter design that decodes nearly twice as fast as the quality-only autoresearch reference while using 35\% less inference memory. In a later campaign with its own benchmark, a Claude Opus 5.5 agent found Transformers with 22 to 26 million parameters and rewrote their generation code with the established techniques of CUDA graph replay and kernel fusion, reaching [-up to 31.8-]{+approximately 32+} times the scored decode-only throughput of its registered baseline. Pre-registered retraining on new random seeds showed why the time budget favoured these designs: they processed more data within the time limit but did not learn more from each training token. Autonomous agents can thus discover substantial system efficiencies, and the objectives and budgets we set shape which solutions they pursue.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} autonomous ML research; neural architecture search; Transformer efficiency; multi-objective optimization; reproducibility
