\section{Autonomous research result}\label{partone}

\subsection{The question}\label{sec:question}
{+\looseness=-1+} In Karpathy's autoresearch \citep{autoresearch}, an AI agent repeatedly modifies a language model's training program, trains each version for the same 300 seconds on one GPU and uses the measured quality to choose its next modification. A program that runs faster therefore also trains on more data. AutoPareto keeps this loop but scores decoding speed and inference memory alongside quality. \textbf{We ask whether agents under such a budget find models that learn better or models that train faster, and whether their gains survive once this coupling is removed.}

{+\looseness=-1+} \textbf{Terms.} Quality is measured in \emph{bits per byte} (BPB), the average number of bits the model needs to encode each byte of unseen text, so lower is better. Agents saw \emph{validation BPB}. We confirm results on \emph{held-out BPB}, computed on a pinned shard that no agent saw. A model trains on \emph{tokens}, pieces of text, and changes its weights once per batch in an \emph{optimizer update}, so halving the batch doubles the updates for the same tokens. A model's \emph{exposure} is the number of tokens it trained on. [-\needspace{3\baselineskip}\textbf{Two-]{+\textbf{Two+} training contracts.} A \emph{fixed-time contract} gives every candidate 300\,s of training, so faster code processes more tokens and makes more updates. A \emph{fixed-token contract} gives every candidate the same number of tokens, however long training takes. Appendix~\ref{app:glossary} is a glossary.

\subsection{How AutoPareto works}\label{sec:brief}
{+\looseness=-1+} \textbf{The human sets the rules.} The task is to train a small decoder-only Transformer language model on web text (ClimbMix) for 300\,s on one NVIDIA A40, starting from a model with 8 layers, width 512 and 50.3M parameters. Candidates are scored on validation BPB, on \emph{decode throughput} (tokens per second when generating one token at a time) and on peak generation memory. The agent may edit only \texttt{train.py}, which holds the model, optimizer, training loop and, later, the inference code.

{+\looseness=-1+} \textbf{The agent runs the loop} [-(Figure~\ref{fig:loop}).-]{+(Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:loop}).+} In each \emph{attempt} the agent reads[-the hypotheses, code changes and measurements of-] all earlier attempts, picks a \emph{parent} recipe, [-writes down-]{+records+} a hypothesis and [-the trade-off it expects, and only-]{+its expected trade-off,+} then edits \texttt{train.py}. The controller checks that nothing else changed, then trains, measures and records [-the result,-]{+it,+} failures included. Any eligible earlier recipe can be a parent, so the search is a [-tree, not a chain.-]{+tree.+} A recipe is on the \emph{Pareto frontier} if no other recipe is at least as good on all three objectives and better on one. {+A recipe can thus stay on the frontier by being the most accurate, fastest or smallest, without winning everywhere.+}

